      "bodyTr": "The Democratic Party's task force for impeaching Chief Justice Cho Hee-dae has announced it will not make the Supreme Court's ruling on former Prime Minister Han Duck-soo a grounds for impeachment. This apparent decision stems from the recognition that including judicial outcomes as impeachment grounds would inevitably invite criticism of violating judicial independence. Instead, the task force cited procedural defects in the trial proceedings, such as changing the sentencing date from the agreed trial date in Han's case and failing to issue a ruling within three months. The party has also added the Supreme Court's decision to reverse and remand the case of President Lee Jae-myung on public election law violations before the presidential election and controversies over reappointment of justices as impeachment grounds. While the Democratic Party is marshaling various grounds since current law precludes judicial rulings as impeachment causes, it is evident that the driving force behind their push for impeachment is the Supreme Court's full bench decision to reverse and remand Han's appeal with partial acquittal implications.\n\nPreviously, when the Jo Kuk Innovation Party strongly demanded impeachment proceedings against Chief Justice Cho, the Democratic Party maintained an extremely cautious stance, essentially shelving national investigation initiatives. However, the party's position shifted markedly after the Supreme Court ruling. Party leader Kim Min-seok criticized it as \"historical betrayal,\" and Rep. Yang Bu-nam, head of the impeachment task force, stated that \"the Han Duck-soo ruling has pushed patience to its limits\" and announced plans to pass an impeachment bill by year's end. The ruling bloc's true intent in pursuing impeachment of the chief justice can only be understood as dissatisfaction with the judicial outcome. When those in power reject court decisions and use them as grounds to impeach the chief justice, this severely undermines public trust in the judicial system and directly violates separation of powers. If the Democratic Party, with its parliamentary majority, processes an impeachment bill, Chief Justice Cho's duties would be suspended. Some ruling party members have already argued that impeachment is necessary to prevent Chief Justice Cho from exercising his authority over regular judicial appointments in February next year.\n\nThe opposition plans to continue outdoor rallies condemning impeachment of the chief justice and pursuit of a special prosecutor for alleged prosecutorial misconduct, beginning with a gathering in Daegu the day before yesterday. Impeachment of the chief justice could invite extreme confrontation between political camps and sow seeds of national division. The Democratic Party should ask itself: Can it truly be considered proper to shake the foundation of constitutional order—the separation of powers—by pursuing impeachment on flimsy grounds that contradict the true motive of judicial non-compliance, all while running counter to public sentiment?",
